he **order questions were asked**.  \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-34-> - If you have a repeated task, its outcome must be the **last element**.  \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd:35:> - For base tasks (all but last), the function reads the **digits** in each name as the task id (e.g., `\"choice4\"`, `\"Q4\"`, `\"task04\"` → task 4).  \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-36-> - The **repeated base task** is inferred from the **first base outcome’s digits**. The repeated outcome itself **need not** contain digits—only its position (last) matters.  \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd:37:> - Outcome strings should end with your choice labels; by default we parse the **last character** and expect `\"A\"`/`\"B\"`. If your survey uses `\"1\"`/`\"2\"` (or other endings), set `.choice_labels` accordingly.\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-38-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-39-**Examples**\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-40-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-41-```{r}\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-42-# Standard order; repeated = task 1\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-43-data(\"exampleData1\")\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd:44:outcomes <- c(paste0(\"choice\", 1:8), \"choice1_repeated_flipped\")\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd:45:out1 <- reshape_projoint(exampleData1, outcomes)\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-46-```\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-47-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-48----\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-49-\n--\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-78-Suppose you want to estimate, within a given profile pair, the probability of choosing a profile that includes \"40% of pre-tax income\" (`level3`) for Housing Cost (`att1`) rather than one that includes \"15% of pre-tax income\" (`level1`) for the same attribute, averaging over all combinations of the other attributes and across respondents; then use the following code:\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-79-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-80-```{r}\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-81-qoi <- set_qoi(\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd:82:  .structure = \"choice_level\",\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-83-  .att_choose = \"att1\",\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-84-  .lev_choose = \"level3\",\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-85-  .att_notchoose = \"att1\",\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-86-  .lev_notchoose = \"level1\"\n--\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-93-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-94-</details>\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-95----\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-96-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd:97:## 📉 Estimate AMCEs\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-98-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd:99:<details><summary><b>Choice-Level AMCEs (Specific Level)</b></summary>\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-100-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd:101:Suppose you want to quantify how the choice probability **changes** between the following profile pairs:\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-102-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-103-- choosing a profile that includes \"40% of pre-tax income\" (`level3`) for Housing Cost (`att1`) versus one that includes \"15% of pre-tax income\" (`level1`) for Housing Cost (`att1`); **and**\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-104-- [baseline] choosing a profile that includes \"30% of pre-tax income\" (`level2`) for Housing Cost (`att1`) versus one that includes \"15% of pre-tax income\" (`level1`) for Housing Cost (`att1`); \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-105-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-106-averaging over all combinations of the other attributes and across respondents. Then write the following code:\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-107-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-108-```{r}\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-109-qoi <- set_qoi(\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd:110:  .structure = \"choice_level\",\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-111-  .estimand = \"amce\",\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-112-  .att_choose = \"att1\",\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-113-  .lev_choose = \"level3\",\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-114-  .att_notchoose = \"att1\",\n--\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-126-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-127-</details>\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-128----\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-129-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd:130:## 🔎 Predict Intra-Respondent Reliability (IRR)\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-131-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-132-If your design **does not include** a repeated task, you can **predict** IRR using predict_tau(), based on observed respondent behavior.\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-133-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-134-<details><summary><b>Predict IRR Using `predict_tau()`</b></summary>\n--\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-167-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-168-data(out1_arranged, package = \"projoint\")\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-169-```\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-170-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd:171:## 🎨 Visualize MMs or AMCEs\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-172-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-173-The **projoint** package provides ready-to-publish plotting tools for conjoint analysis results.\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-174-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd:175:<span style=\"color:DarkRed\"><b>Note:</b></span> The current version of **projoint** supports plotting choice-level MMs only.  \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd:176:Support for **choice-level AMCEs** will be available in future updates!\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-177-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-178----\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-179-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-180-### ⚖️ Choice-Level Analysis\n--\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-184-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-185-- Specify your quantity of interest:\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-186-```{r}\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-187-qoi_mm <- set_qoi(\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd:188:  .structure = \"choice_level\", # default\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-189-  .att_choose = \"att1\", \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-190-  .lev_choose = \"level1\", \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-191-  .att_notchoose = \"att1\", \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-192-  .lev_notchoose = \"level3\"\n--\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-194-```\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-195-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-196-- Estimate\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-197-```{r}\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd:198:choice_mm <- projoint(\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-199-  .data = out1_arranged, \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-200-  .qoi = qoi_mm, \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-201-  .ignore_position = TRUE\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-202-)\n--\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-206-<details>\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-207-<summary><b>Visualize (Levels) </b></summary>\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-208-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-209-```{r, fig.width = 6, fig.height = 3}\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd:210:plot(choice_mm)\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-211-```\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-212-</details>\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-213-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-214-<details>\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-215-<summary><b>Visualize (Differences)</b></summary>\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-216-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-217-```{r, fig.width = 8, fig.height = 2}\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd:218:plot(choice_mm, .type = \"pointrange\")\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-219-```\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-220-</details>\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-221-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd-222----\n--\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-1----\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-2-title: \"Choice-Level Analysis\"\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-3-output: rmarkdown::html_vignette\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-4-description: >\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd:5:  Why choice-level analysis offers deeper insights compared to standard profile-level analysis.\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-6-vignette: >\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-7-  %\\VignetteIndexEntry{Choice-Level Analysis}\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-8-  %\\VignetteEngine{knitr::rmarkdown}\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-9-  %\\VignetteEncoding{UTF-8}\n--\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-13-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-14-💡 **Choice-level analysis is simpler, easier, and more powerful than profile-level analysis.**\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-15-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-16-- 🧠 Conjoint designs began in market research and psychology, where respondents **rated** two profiles (e.g., products). Each rating was treated as an **independent observation**, creating the **profile-level design** with `2 × n` rows for `n` respondents. \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd:17:- 🔍 Social scientists later used conjoint designs for **choices** instead of ratings but kept the same structure with a **single binary outcome**. This shift introduced **statistical and conceptual issues**.\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-18-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-19----\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-20-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-21-### ⚠️ **Problems with Profile-Level Analysis**\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-22-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-23-🚫 Profile-level analysis forces researchers to correct a dependence that they created themselves.\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-24-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd:25:- 🔁 **Redundant structure:** Each choice task generates **two rows** per task for each respondent even though there is only **one independent choice**.  \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-26-- 🔗 **Mechanical dependence:** Selecting one profile **necessarily implies rejecting the other**, violating the independence assumption.  \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-27-- 🧩 **Artificial complexity:** Analysts must correct for this dependence using **complicated statistical adjustments**, even though it arises solely from how the data were organized—not from respondents’ behavior.\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-28-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-29-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-30----\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-31-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-32-### ✅ **Advantages of Choice-Level Analysis**\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-33-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd:34:In contrast, **choice-level analysis** organizes data by respondent *decisions* rather than profiles.\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-35-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd:36:- 📋 Each unit of observation represents **a choice** for a given task per respondent.  \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-37-- ⚡ The outcome variable reflects **which profile was chosen** in that task.  \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd:38:- 🧠 This data structure allows researchers to model the choice **conditional on the full comparison**.\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-39-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-40-🎯 **Choice-level analysis** directly models the respondent’s decision between two (or more) alternatives,  \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-41-capturing the true structure of the conjoint task.\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-42-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-43----\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-44-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-45-## **Key Issues and Applications**\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-46-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd:47:- Profile-level estimands like **AMCEs** assume that each profile is generated independently and ignores how respondents evaluate one profile *relative* to another.  This limits the types of questions researchers can ask.\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-48-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-49-- Choice-level analysis allows researchers to explore questions that **explicitly depend on the comparison between profiles**, such as:\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-50-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-51-<details>\n--\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-82-- 🧠 **Real-world decision-making**, where people choose among competing alternatives.  \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-83-- 🔄 **Comparative cognition**, where evaluations depend on the context of available options.  \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-84-- 🎛️ **Tradeoff reasoning**, where respondents weigh attribute combinations jointly.\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-85-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd:86:Hence, rather than estimating the probability of selecting an isolated profile, choice-level analysis estimates the **probability of choosing one profile over another**, conditional on all attributes involved.\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-87-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-88-✅ Mirrors real-world behavior  \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-89-✅ Captures comparative judgment and psychological context  \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-90-✅ Reveals authentic tradeoffs and priorities  \n--\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-105-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-106-## **Key Takeaway**\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-107-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-108-🚀 If your conjoint design presents respondents with **two or more profiles for comparison**,  \n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd:109:then **choice-level analysis is essential for valid, interpretable, and psychologically realistic inference**.\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-110-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-111-It provides:\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-112-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/structure.Rmd-113-- **Deeper insights into human decision-making**  \n--\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/design.Rmd-27-The following instructions are taken directly from the Projoint Survey Design Tool's <a href=\"https://projoint.aaronrkaufman.com/tutorials/00_tutorial.md\" target=\"_blank\">Tutorial section</a>.\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/design.Rmd-28-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/design.Rmd-29-<details style=\"margin-left: 25px; margin-bottom: -10px\">\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/design.Rmd-30-<summary style=\"font-size: 18px;\">**1. Setting the Attributes**</summary>\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/design.Rmd:31:The most important part of a discrete choice experiment is setting the attributes and levels: these are the features that are getting randomized. Add attributes by clicking on the \"Add Attribute\" button, and add levels by clicking on the \"Add Level\" button.\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/design.Rmd-32-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/design.Rmd-33-There are two advanced options on the page where users add attributes and levels. The first controls the order in which attributes appear to respondents. By clicking on the padlock icon to the right of each attribute, the user can control where they appear. A locked icon will always appear in the indicated position, so if the second attribute is locked, that attribute will always appear second in the list of attributes shown to respondents.\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/design.Rmd-34-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/design.Rmd:35:The second advanced option controls randomization weights, and can be accesed by clicking on the \"Edit Weights\" button. This allows researchers to set how frequently each level should appear. Most choice experiments set each level to equal frequency, but this is not necessarily preferred.\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/design.Rmd-36-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/design.Rmd-37-After you have set the attributes and levels, we recommend going to the Preview screen to preview your study. This produces a single task, which can be refreshed, so the researcher can see what the respondents will see.\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/design.Rmd-38-\n/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/design.Rmd-39-</details>\n--\n\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc \"PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 -c '\nimport bz2,struct\nb=bz2.open(\\\"/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/data/exampleData1.rda\\\",\\\"rb\\\").read();p=19;refs=[]\ndef I():\n global p\n z=struct.unpack(\\\">i\\\",b[p:p+4])[0];p+=4;return z\ndef S():\n global p\n f=I();t=f&255;attr=f&512\n if t==255:return refs[(f>>8)-1]\n if t==254:return None\n o=[t,None,None];refs.append(o)\n if t==0:pass\n elif t==1:o[1]=S()\n elif t==9:\n  n=I();o[1]=None if n<0 else b[p:p+n].decode();p+=max(n,0)\n elif t in(10,13):n=I();o[1]=[I() for z in range(n)]\n elif t==14:\n  n=I();o[1]=list(struct.unpack(\\\">\\\"+\\\"d\\\"*n,b[p:p+8*n]));p+=8*n\n elif t==24:n=I();o[1]=b[p:p+n];p+=n\n elif t in(16,19,20):n=I();o[1]=[S() for z in range(n)]\n elif t in(2,17,6):o[1]=S();o[2]=S();o.append(S())\n else: raise Exception((t,hex(f),p))\n if attr:o.append(S())\n return o\ndef ss(x):return x[1] if x[0]==9 else ss(x[1]) if x[0]==1 else str(x)\ndef pl(x):\n a=[]\n whi