d_flipped\\\")); print(out\"'$labels, n=Inf); cat(\"'\"\\\\ncorrection explanation\\\\n\\\"); cat(readLines(\\\"/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/projoint/doc/analyze.Rmd\\\")[19:95],sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\")'\" in /Users/scdenney/Documents/github/resources/ai-for-research/demos/orchestration-lab/runs/46-sol-v2/t3\n succeeded in 930ms:\n# A tibble: 24 × 4\n   attribute                                         level attribute_id level_id\n   <chr>                                             <chr> <chr>        <chr>   \n 1 Housing Cost                                      15% … att1         att1:le…\n 2 Housing Cost                                      30% … att1         att1:le…\n 3 Housing Cost                                      40% … att1         att1:le…\n 4 Presidential Vote (2020)                          30% … att2         att2:le…\n 5 Presidential Vote (2020)                          50% … att2         att2:le…\n 6 Presidential Vote (2020)                          70% … att2         att2:le…\n 7 Racial Composition                                50% … att3         att3:le…\n 8 Racial Composition                                75% … att3         att3:le…\n 9 Racial Composition                                90% … att3         att3:le…\n10 Racial Composition                                96% … att3         att3:le…\n11 School Quality                                    5 ou… att4         att4:le…\n12 School Quality                                    9 ou… att4         att4:le…\n13 Total Daily Driving Time for Commuting and Erran… 10 m… att5         att5:le…\n14 Total Daily Driving Time for Commuting and Erran… 25 m… att5         att5:le…\n15 Total Daily Driving Time for Commuting and Erran… 45 m… att5         att5:le…\n16 Total Daily Driving Time for Commuting and Erran… 75 m… att5         att5:le…\n17 Type of Place                                     City… att6         att6:le…\n18 Type of Place                                     City… att6         att6:le…\n19 Type of Place                                     Rura… att6         att6:le…\n20 Type of Place                                     Smal… att6         att6:le…\n21 Type of Place                                     Subu… att6         att6:le…\n22 Type of Place                                     Subu… att6         att6:le…\n23 Violent Crime Rate (Vs National Rate)             20% … att7         att7:le…\n24 Violent Crime Rate (Vs National Rate)             20% … att7         att7:le…\n\ncorrection explanation\n## 🎯 Estimate Corrected MMs or AMCEs\n\nIn conjoint analysis, default MMs and AMCEs can be **biased** due to measurement error from intra-respondent variability.\n\n**projoint** corrects for this bias automatically.\n\nThe following instructions apply to choice-level data. What if you have profile-level data?<sup><a href=\"faq.html#what-if-i-have-profile-level-data\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"text-decoration: none;\">&#9432;</a></sup> Our <a href=\"faq.html\" target=\"_blank\">FAQ Page</a> has instructions to estimate and visualize profile-level QOIs.\n\n---\n\n## 📦 Prepare Example Data\n\n> **Outcome naming & order (important)**\n> \n> - List `.outcomes` in the **order questions were asked**.  \n> - If you have a repeated task, its outcome must be the **last element**.  \n> - For base tasks (all but last), the function reads the **digits** in each name as the task id (e.g., `\"choice4\"`, `\"Q4\"`, `\"task04\"` → task 4).  \n> - The **repeated base task** is inferred from the **first base outcome’s digits**. The repeated outcome itself **need not** contain digits—only its position (last) matters.  \n> - Outcome strings should end with your choice labels; by default we parse the **last character** and expect `\"A\"`/`\"B\"`. If your survey uses `\"1\"`/`\"2\"` (or other endings), set `.choice_labels` accordingly.\n\n**Examples**\n\n```{r}\n# Standard order; repeated = task 1\ndata(\"exampleData1\")\noutcomes <- c(paste0(\"choice\", 1:8), \"choice1_repeated_flipped\")\nout1 <- reshape_projoint(exampleData1, outcomes)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🛠️ Why Use IDs (e.g., `att1`, `level1`)?\n\nBefore estimating quantities, it’s important to understand how attribute and level IDs work inside projoint.\n\n<details>\nWe recommend working with **attribute IDs** rather than actual text labels because:\n\n- Safer against special characters, languages, or typos\n- Allows **multiple attributes** to have identical labels (e.g., \"High\" for both \"Teaching Quality\" and \"Research Quality\")\n\nCheck attribute-level mappings:\n\n```{r}\nout1$labels\n```\n\nYou can also save these labels for easier editing:\n\n```r\nsave_labels(out1, \"labels.csv\")\n```\n</details>\n---\n\n## 📈 Estimate Marginal Means (MMs)\n\n<details><summary><b>Choice-Level MMs (Specific Level)</b></summary>\n\nSuppose you want to estimate, within a given profile pair, the probability of choosing a profile that includes \"40% of pre-tax income\" (`level3`) for Housing Cost (`att1`) rather than one that includes \"15% of pre-tax income\" (`level1`) for the same attribute, averaging over all combinations of the other attributes and across respondents; then use the following code:\n\n```{r}\nqoi <- set_qoi(\n  .structure = \"choice_level\",\n  .att_choose = \"att1\",\n  .lev_choose = \"level3\",\n  .att_notchoose = \"att1\",\n  .lev_notchoose = \"level1\"\n)\n\nmm2 <- projoint(out1, qoi)\nprint(mm2)\nsummary(mm2)\n```\n\n</details>\n---\n\ncodex\nThe reshaped data contain 6,400 profiles (400 respondents × 8 binary-choice tasks) and identify crime as `att7` with exactly two levels. The package’s profile-level estimator automatically clusters at respondent `id` (CR2 for MMs; its documented fallback is used where AMCE CR2 is non-PSD).\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc \"Rscript -e 'library(projoint); library(dplyr); data(exampleData1); out<-reshape_projoint(exampleData1,.outcomes=c(paste0(\\\"choice\\\",1:8),\\\"choice1_repeated_flipped\\\")); labs<-as.data.frame(out\"'$labels); options(width=1000); print(labs, row.names=FALSE); m<-projoint(out,.structure=\"profile_level\",.estimand=\"mm\")$estimates %>% filter(estimand==\"mm_corrected\") %>% left_join(labs, by=c(\"att_level_choose\"=\"level_id\")) %>% select(attribute,level,estimate,se,conf.low,conf.high); cat(\"'\"\\\\nCORRECTED MMs\\\\n\\\"); print(as.data.frame(m),row.names=FALSE); cat(\\\"\\\\nMM RANGES\\\\n\\\"); print(m%>%group_by(attribute)%>%summarise(min=min(estimate),max=max(estimate),range=max-min,.groups=\\\"drop\\\"),n=Inf); est <- function(att,lev,base) { q<-set_qoi(.structure=\\\"profile_level\\\",.estimand=\\\"amce\\\",.att_choose=att,.lev_choose=lev,.att_choose_b=att,.lev_choose_b=base); projoint(out,.qoi=q)\"'$estimates %>% filter(estimand==\"amce_corrected\") %>% mutate(chosen=lev,baseline=base) }; crime<-bind_rows(est(\"att7\",\"level2\",\"level1\"),est(\"att7\",\"level1\",\"level2\")) %>% left_join(labs%>%select(level,level_id),by=c(\"chosen\"=\"level_id\"))%>%rename(chosen_label=level)%>%left_join(labs%>%select(level,level_id),by=c(\"baseline\"=\"level_id\"))%>%rename(baseline_label=level)%>%select(chosen_label,baseline_label,estimate,se,conf.low,conf.high); cat(\"'\"\\\\nCRIME AMCE corrected\\\\n\\\"); print(as.data.frame(crime),row.names=FALSE); housing<-bind_rows(est(\\\"att1\\\",\\\"level2\\\",\\\"level1\\\"),est(\\\"att1\\\",\\\"level3\\\",\\\"level1\\\"),est(\\\"att1\\\",\\\"level1\\\",\\\"level2\\\"),est(\\\"att1\\\",\\\"level3\\\",\\\"level2\\\"),est(\\\"att1\\\",\\\"level1\\\",\\\"level3\\\"),est(\\\"att1\\\",\\\"level2\\\",\\\"level3\\\")) %>% left_join(labs%>%select(level,level_id),by=c(\\\"chosen\\\"=\\\"level_id\\\"))%>%rename(chosen_label=level)%>%left_join(labs%>%select(level,level_id),by=c(\\\"baseline\\\"=\\\"level_id\\\"))%>%rename(baseline_label=level)%>%select(chosen_label,baseline_label,estimate,se,conf.low,conf.high);cat(\\\"\\\\nHOUSING AMCE corrected\\\\n\\\"); print(as.data.frame(housing),row.names=FALSE); cat(\\\"\\\\nSE METADATA\\\\n\\\"); print(c(mm=projoint(out,.structure=\\\"profile_level\\\",.estimand=\\\"mm\\\")\"'$se_type_used,amce=projoint(out,.structure=\"profile_level\",.estimand=\"amce\")$se_type_used));'\"'\" in /Users/scdenney/Documents/github/resources/ai-for-research/demos/orchestration-lab/runs/46-sol-v2/t3\n succeeded in 3062ms:\n\nAttaching package: ‘dplyr’\n\nThe following objects are masked from ‘package:stats’:\n\n    filter, lag\n\nThe following objects are masked from ‘package:base’:\n\n    intersect, setdiff, setequal, union\n\n                                          attribute                                                         level attribute_id    level_id\n                                       Housing Cost                                         15% of pre-tax income         att1 att1:level1\n                                       Housing Cost                                         30% of pre-tax income         att1 att1:level2\n                                       Housing Cost                                         40% of pre-tax income         att1 att1:level3\n                           Presidential Vote (2020)                                  30% Democrat, 70% Republican         att2 att2:level1\n                           Presidential Vote (2020)                                  50% Democrat, 50% Republican         att2 att2:level2\n                           Presidential Vote (2020)                                  70% Democrat, 30% Republican         att2 att2:level3\n                                 Racial Composition                                       50% White, 50% Nonwhite         att3 att3:level1\n                                 Racial Composition                                       75% White, 25% Nonwhite         att3 att3:level2\n                                 Racial Composition                                       90% White, 10% Nonwhite         att3 att3:level3\n                                 Racial Composition                                        96% White