/NA variances; fell back to se_type='stata' (then HC1 if needed).\n\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/scdenney/Documents/github/resources/open-science-skills/codex/advisor/scripts/sol-advisor.sh --check' in /Users/scdenney/Documents/github/resources/ai-for-research/demos/orchestration-lab/runs/46-sol-v2/t3\n succeeded in 0ms:\nWARNING: proceeding, even though we could not create PATH aliases: Operation not permitted (os error 1)\ncodex-cli 0.144.6\n\n2026-07-19T08:16:54.367310Z ERROR codex_core::tools::router: error=patch rejected: writing outside of the project; rejected by user approval settings\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /Users/scdenney/Documents/github/resources/open-science-skills/codex/advisor/scripts/sol-advisor.sh\" in /Users/scdenney/Documents/github/resources/ai-for-research/demos/orchestration-lab/runs/46-sol-v2/t3\n succeeded in 0ms:\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# sol-advisor.sh — consult GPT-5.6 \"Sol\" as an independent advisor (Sol/high by default).\n#\n# Read-only advisory consult, not implementation. Spawns a fresh, ephemeral\n# `codex exec` session (--sandbox read-only: no edits) at the explicit\n# effort selected by the library policy: Sol/high, always. See SKILL.md.\n#\n# This spawns an ISOLATED session with no automatic access to the calling\n# conversation, so the caller must compose a self-contained briefing and\n# pass it as --prompt-file.\n#\n# SANDBOX WARNING (confirmed by direct reproduction, July 2026): this script\n# nests a `codex exec` process inside whatever process runs it. If the\n# CALLER is itself a sandboxed `codex exec`/Codex session, this nested call\n# fails immediately with \"failed to initialize in-process app-server\n# client: Operation not permitted\" (macOS) or \"Read-only file system\"\n# (Linux) -- an OS-level sandbox applies transitively to the whole process\n# tree, and passing --dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox to THIS\n# script does not help, since the restriction is imposed on the parent, not\n# requested by the child. Only an unsandboxed caller, or an interactive\n# caller that requests escalation (sandbox_permissions: require_escalated)\n# for this specific call, can succeed. See SKILL.md's \"Sandbox constraint\"\n# section before assuming this \"just works.\"\n#\n# Usage:\n#   sol-advisor.sh --prompt-file FILE --out FILE [-C DIR] [--model ID]\n#                   [--effort LEVEL] [--timeout SEC]\n#   sol-advisor.sh --check\n#\n#   --prompt-file FILE  the self-contained briefing (task, progress, question)\n#   --out FILE          where Sol's advice is written\n#   -C DIR              working dir Sol sees (default: $PWD)\n#   --model ID          Codex model to pin (default: gpt-5.6-sol — the\n#                        flagship 5.6 tier, confirmed WORKING as of July 2026\n#                        on ChatGPT-account Codex auth; an earlier \"rejected\n#                        outright\" finding no longer reproduces — re-check\n#                        `codex --version` before assuming a gate if it ever\n#                        errors, since an outdated CLI rejects sol/luna too,\n#                        with a different error). sol/terra/luna are three\n#                        distinct tiers, not one gpt-5.6 model. Pass --model\n#                        gpt-5.6-terra explicitly for a cheaper reviewer on\n#                        routine consults.\n#   --effort LEVEL      none|minimal|low|medium|high|xhigh (default: xhigh)\n#   --timeout SEC        hard kill after SEC seconds (default: 900)\nset -euo pipefail\n\nMODEL=\"gpt-5.6-sol\"\nWORKDIR=\"$PWD\"\nPROMPT_FILE=\"\"\nOUT=\"\"\nEFFORT=\"xhigh\"\nTIMEOUT_SECONDS=900\n\nusage() {\n  printf '%s\\n' \\\n    'Usage: sol-advisor.sh --prompt-file FILE --out FILE [-C DIR] [--model ID] [--effort LEVEL] [--timeout SEC]' \\\n    '       sol-advisor.sh --check'\n}\n\ndie() { printf 'sol-advisor: %s\\n' \"$*\" >&2; exit 2; }\n\nif [[ \"${1:-}\" == \"--check\" ]]; then\n  command -v codex >/dev/null || die 'codex CLI not found'\n  codex --version\n  exit 0\nfi\n\nwhile [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do\n  case \"$1\" in\n    --prompt-file) PROMPT_FILE=\"${2:-}\"; shift 2 ;;\n    --out) OUT=\"${2:-}\"; shift 2 ;;\n    -C) WORKDIR=\"${2:-}\"; shift 2 ;;\n    --model) MODEL=\"${2:-}\"; shift 2 ;;\n    --effort) EFFORT=\"${2:-}\"; shift 2 ;;\n    --timeout) TIMEOUT_SECONDS=\"${2:-}\"; shift 2 ;;\n    -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;\n    *) die \"unknown argument: $1\" ;;\n  esac\ndone\n\ncommand -v codex >/dev/null || die 'codex CLI not found'\n[[ -n \"$PROMPT_FILE\" && -r \"$PROMPT_FILE\" ]] || die 'readable --prompt-file is required (the self-contained briefing)'\n[[ -n \"$OUT\" ]] || die '--out is required'\n[[ -d \"$WORKDIR\" ]] || die \"working directory not found: $WORKDIR\"\n[[ \"$TIMEOUT_SECONDS\" =~ ^[1-9][0-9]*$ ]] || die '--timeout must be a positive integer'\ncase \"$EFFORT\" in none|minimal|low|medium|high|xhigh) ;; *) die \"bad --effort: $EFFORT (none|minimal|low|medium|high|xhigh)\" ;; esac\ncase \"$PROMPT_FILE\" in /*) ;; *) PROMPT_FILE=\"$PWD/$PROMPT_FILE\" ;; esac\ncase \"$OUT\" in /*) ;; *) OUT=\"$PWD/$OUT\" ;; esac\nWORKDIR=\"$(cd \"$WORKDIR\" && pwd -P)\"\nmkdir -p \"$(dirname \"$OUT\")\"\n\ncmd=(codex exec --ephemeral --model \"$MODEL\" -c model_reasoning_effort=\"$EFFORT\" --sandbox read-only --skip-git-repo-check -C \"$WORKDIR\" --output-last-message \"$OUT\" \\\n  'You are consulted as an independent GPT-5.6 advisor for one specific decision point — not a co-implementer. Read the self-contained briefing supplied on stdin (the task, what has been done so far, the current approach or findings, and the specific question). You have no access to the original conversation beyond this briefing, so if it seems to be missing something you need, say what is missing rather than guessing. Give direct, decisive advice on the specific question asked. If you disagree with the stated approach, say so plainly and explain the specific failure mode, do not hedge into a survey of options. Do not edit any files — you are read-only advisory only. Return only your advice, no preamble.')\n\nif command -v timeout >/dev/null; then\n  timeout \"${TIMEOUT_SECONDS}s\" \"${cmd[@]}\" < \"$PROMPT_FILE\" >/dev/null\nelse\n  \"${cmd[@]}\" < \"$PROMPT_FILE\" >/dev/null\nfi\n\n[[ -s \"$OUT\" ]] || die 'Sol returned no output'\n\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc \"Rscript -e 'writeLines(c(\\\"Independent advisory request: assess the defensible substantive claim for a profile-level conjoint analysis of projoint::exampleData1 reshaped with 400 respondent IDs x 8 choice tasks = 6,400 profiles. The package projoint 1.1.1 uses automatic respondent clustering. Corrected MMs: lower crime (20% less than national) .6255815 (SE .0211228; 95% CI .5840389,.6671241); higher crime (20% more) .3744185 (same SE; CI .3328759,.4159611). Corrected AMCE lower versus higher = +.2511630 (SE .0422455; CI .1680777,.3342482); reversing the baseline gives -.2511630 exactly. Crime is binary. Corrected MM ranges by attribute: housing .198072; presidential vote .0557455; racial composition .059281; school quality .115675; driving time .237268; type of place .157810; crime .251163. Housing MMs: 15%=.6135867, 30%=.4765303, 40%=.4155147; AMCE 30 vs 15=-.1370564, 40 vs 15=-.1980720; releveling produces exact inverses and 40 vs 30=-.0610156. What should a 400-word reply concede and claim? Avoid causal and cross-attribute importance overclaims.\\\"), \\\"/private/tmp/projoint-advisor-brief.txt\\\")' && /Users/scdenney/Documents/github/resources/open-science-skills/codex/advisor/scripts/sol-advisor.sh --prompt-file /private/tmp/projoint-advisor-brief.txt --out /private/tmp/projoint-advisor-out.txt -C \\\"\"'$PWD\" --model gpt-5.6-sol --effort xhigh --timeout 60' in /Users/scdenney/Documents/github/resources/ai-for-research/demos/orchestration-lab/runs/46-sol-v2/t3\n exited 1 in 170ms:\nWARNING: proceeding, even though we could not create PATH aliases: Operation not permitted (os error 1)\nReading additional input from stdin...\nError: failed to initialize in-process app-server client: Operation not permitted (os error 1)\n\nturn interrupted\ntokens used\n48,655\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"ls -la terra-analysis.txt claude-peer.txt 2>&1; sed -n '1,260p' claude-peer.txt\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"ls -la terra-analysis.txt claude-peer.txt 2>&1; sed -n '1,260p' claude-peer.txt\"","aggregated_output":"ls: terra-analysis.txt: No such file or directory\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 scdenney  staff  6454 Jul 19 10:16 claude-peer.txt\nAll computations done. Here is my independent adjudication.\n\n---\n\n## Verdict\n\n**The reviewer's mechanical premise is largely wrong for this attribute, but the conclusion they stumble toward is right for a different reason.** Violent Crime Rate is binary, so its AMCE is baseline-invariant up to sign: flipping the reference exactly negates the estimate (−0.251 → +0.251) with identical magnitude, SE, and CI width. No baseline choice can make \"the effect of crime look quite different.\" However, the manuscript's headline — that crime *drives* community choice — is still not supported: on the baseline-invariant marginal-means scale, crime's spread (0.251) is statistically indistinguishable from commute time's (0.237) and not clearly separable from housing cost's (0.198). The headline should be softened from dominance to co-leadership.\n\n## Evidence\n\nAll estimates from `projoint` profile-level analysis with the package's IRR/measurement-error correction, on the exact `reshape_projoint` call in the brief (400 respondents, 6,400 profile rows).\n\n**Corrected marginal means and per-attribute spreads (max − min):**\n\n| Attribute | Levels | Max MM | Min MM | Spread |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Violent Crime Rate | 2 | 0.626 (20% less) | 0.374 (20% more) | **0.251** |\n| Commute time | 4 | 0.610 (10 min) | 0.373 (75 min) | **0.237** |\n| Housing Cost | 3 | 0.614 (15%) | 0.416 (40%) | 0.198 |\n| Type of Place | 6 | 0.552 (small town) | 0.394 (downtown) | 0.158 |\n| School Quality | 2 | 0.558 | 0.442 | 0.116 |\n| Racial Composition | 4 | 0.537 | 0.477 | 0.059 |\n| Presidential Vote | 3 | 0.536 | 0.480 | 0.056 |\n\nCrime MMs: 0.626 [0.584, 0.667] for less crime, 0.374 [0.333, 0.416] for more crime (SE 0.021 each). Both CIs exclude 0.5.\n\n**Baseline sensitivity, computed directly:**\n- Crime AMCE, default baseline (less crime): −0.251 [−0.334, −0.168]. Flipped baseline (more crime): +0.251 [+0.168, +0.334]. Sign flip only — mechanically forced for any binary attribute.\n- Type of Place (6 levels), the multi-level check: against the downtown baseline all five AMCEs are positive (up to +0.158 for small town); against the small-town baseline all five are negative (down to −0.158 for downtown). Individual reported contrasts change sign and magnitude — e.g., suburban-houses-only reads +0.079 under one baseline and −0.079 under the other — but every pairwise difference and the maximal within-attribute contrast (0.158) are invariant. This is the reviewer's point working exactly as far as it goes: baselines relabel contrasts, they never touch the fitted cell means.\n\n**The comparison the headline actually rests on** (does crime beat the runner-up?): cluster bootstrap by respondent (2,000 reps) on the tie-removed spread differences —\n- crime − commute: +0.009, 95% CI [−0.066, +0.080] — no separation.\n- crime − housing: +0.035, 95% CI [−0.038, +0.106] — no separation.\n- crime − place: +0.061, 95% CI [−0.021, +0.120] — marginal at best.\n\n(One diagnostic worth noting: raw level means understate spreads because ~1/2 of tasks tie on a binary attribute vs ~1/4 on a 4-level one; projoint's default tie removal handles this, and I verified my bootstrap reproduces projoint's uncorrected MMs exactly — crime 0.582/0.418.)\n\n**Levels caveat, in crime's favor:** max−min is biased *upward* for attributes with more levels, so 2-level crime matching or topping 4- and 6-level attributes' ranges is if anything understated. But crime's range also reflects a design choice (±20% vs national average); \"importance\" here is range-of-shown-levels, not a structural quantity.\n\n## Reviewer reply logic\n\nConcede: AMCEs are indeed contrasts against a chosen reference; for multi-level attributes, individual reported coefficients (and naive \"largest coefficient\" rankings) change with the baseline, as our Type of Place re-estimation shows. Rebut: this cannot affect the crime attribute, which is binary — its AMCE magnitude is baseline-invariant, as re-estimation under the flipped reference confirms (±0.251, identical CI). Then move to the invariant scale: marginal means, which involve no reference category, reproduce the finding — the two crime levels are the most extreme cell means in the entire design (0.626 and 0.374) and both differ from indifference. Concede the deeper point the reviewer didn't quite make: on that invariant scale crime does not statistically dominate commute time or housing cost, so the manuscript will drop the dominance framing.\n\n## Proposed revised claim\n\n> \"A community's violent crime rate has one of the largest effects on choice in our design: moving from 20% above to 20% below the national crime rate raises a profile's corrected choice probability from 0.374 to 0.626, the largest single level contrast among all attributes — a quantity invariant to reference-category coding. Crime, commuting time, and housing cost form a clearly leading tier (MM spreads of 0.251, 0.237, and 0.198), statistically indistinguishable from one another and well above the remaining attributes; racial composition and partisan composition matter least (spreads ≤ 0.06).\"\n\n**Core visual:** a single marginal-means dot-whisker plot, all 24 levels grouped by attribute, sorted by attribute spread, with a reference line at 0.5 — baseline-free by construction, shows crime's levels as the two most extreme points, and makes the crime/commute/housing near-tie visible rather than hidden.\n\n## Residual risk\n\n1. Cross-attribute \"importance\" remains range-dependent: crime's ±20% band and the 10–75 min commute range are designer choices; different level ranges would reorder the tiers. The revised claim should keep \"in our design.\"\n2. Max−min spread mechanically favors many-level attributes, so the tier ordering is conservative for crime — but it is still not a model-free importance measure (a full alternative would be something like attribute-level R² decomposition).\n3. My bootstrap ranks uncorrected tie-removed spreads; the IRR correction rescales attributes near-proportionally so ranking is unaffected, but corrected-scale CIs on the spread *differences* would be somewhat wider — reinforcing, not weakening, the \"indistinguishable\" conclusion.\n4. The corrected crime spread (0.251) depends on projoint's measurement-error model via the repeated task; the uncorrected spread (0.165) tells the same relative story, so nothing in the claim hinges on the correction.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"agent_message","text":"The two independent lines agree on the core result: crime’s binary AMCE can only flip sign, and the all-level MMs put crime at a 25.1-point spread, narrowly ahead of commuting at 23.7. I’m taking one bounded revision now: sharpening the multi-level explanation and removing any implication that the 1.4-point descriptive lead itself proves a statistically unique ranking."}}
